# <%= projectName %>

Web-components SPA walking skeleton scaffolded by keel — hexagonal, framework-free:
the browser and the DOM API are the framework.

## Layout

```
design-system/   — the project's design language: atomic design on top
                   of @rgoussu.dev/planks. Domain-blind, consumed by
                   every context, and NOT a context itself — it is the
                   package the import map deduplicates
platform/
  context/       — the WCCG Context protocol primitives, owned by no
                   context: every module's elements ask through them,
                   the assembly answers through them
modules/
  greeting/      — ONE package per bounded context
    src/
      index.ts        — THE FACADE: contract face, factories, context keys
      elements.ts     — THE REGISTRATION: define<%= '' %>GreetingElements() + the tags
      service.ts      — THE PEER SEAM: what another context may call
      domain/
        contract/     — ports, commands, read models; DOM-less
        core/internal/ — services and stores; unreachable from outside
      user-side/
        elements/     — port-bound custom elements (driving adapters)
      infra/          — driven adapters wrapping browser capabilities
    tests/
application/
  web-app/       — the deployment unit: index.html + main.ts (the
                   assembly point)
```

**One workspace package per context, deliberately.** In a TypeScript
workspace the package graph enforces nothing on its own: an
*undeclared* workspace dependency still resolves (the package manager
hoists members into the root `node_modules`), and TypeScript project
references do not restrict which projects a project may import.
Splitting a context into four packages buys four manifests and no
wall.

What is enforced is the `exports` map. This context publishes exactly
three entry points:

| Specifier                       | Reaches                                      |
| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| `<%= contextPkg %>`          | the facade — contract, factories, context keys |
| `<%= contextPkg %>/elements` | `defineGreetingElements()`                    |
| `<%= contextPkg %>/service`  | the peer seam — another context's only way in  |

Anything deeper is a `TS2307` from `tsc` and an
`ERR_PACKAGE_PATH_NOT_EXPORTED` in the browser. Widening that map is
the single edit that undoes the layout.

Registration lives behind its own entry point rather than the facade
because it is a *side effect*: importing the facade from a DOM-less
program (a domain test, a Node tool) must not define anything, and
the assembly needs to be able to order the definition after the
context provider is listening.

## The element tag prefix

`<scope>-<context>-<element>` — here `<%= viewTag %>`.

This is the one string in the layout that nothing checks. It is not a
type, not a module specifier and not a path: get the prefix wrong and
the build is green, the typecheck is green, the tests are green, and
the page renders an unknown element as an empty inline box. So it has
a consumer — `modules/greeting/tests/element-tags.test.ts` re-derives
the prefix from the package's own name and fails when the two
disagree. Keep that test when you add elements, and add your tag to it.

## The import map

`index.html` carries a `<script type="importmap">` pointing
`<%= designSystemPkg %>` at `/vendor/design-system.js`, and the app's
Vite build leaves it **external**.

This is correctness, not size. A package that defines custom elements
must exist exactly once per page: two bundles each inlining a copy
throw `NotSupportedError: <tag> has already been used with this
registry` on the second registration, and that throw aborts the rest
of that bundle's registrations — so half the page silently stops
upgrading, with no error anyone sees. With a single context there is
one bundle and nothing to collide; the map is here so that carving a
context into its own bundle stays a wiring change rather than a
debugging session.

The root `build` script builds the design system first (workspace
order) and the app's Vite plugin copies its output to `dist/vendor/`,
failing loudly if it is missing.

## Wiring

No mediator. Each use case is a driving port delivered to elements over
the [WCCG Context protocol](https://github.com/webcomponents-cc/community-protocols/blob/main/proposals/context.md):
`main.ts` constructs adapters and domain factories, registers one
`context-request` listener, then calls each context's
`define…Elements()` — provider first, definitions last. Cross-cutting
concerns decorate the factories at the assembly point.

Adding a bounded context is three lines in `main.ts` — its ports, its
context keys, its `define…Elements()` — plus a sibling package under
`modules/`. It reaches this one through
`<%= contextPkg %>/service` and nothing else.

## Design system

Atomic design and the hexagon compose: the hexagon organises the
application, atomic design organises the inside of the driving adapter.
The line through the hierarchy is **port-awareness**:

- **Sub-atomic** — [planks](https://github.com/rgoussu-dev/planks)
  layout primitives (`<stack-pk>`, `<box-pk>`, …) and its token scale,
  plus the project's brand layer in `design-system/src/tokens.css`.
- **Atoms & molecules** — `design-system/src/atoms/`,
  `design-system/src/molecules/`: attributes in, `CustomEvent`s out,
  never a domain import.
- **Port-bound organisms** — `modules/<ctx>/src/user-side/elements/`:
  where ports meet design-system children; they push read-model state
  down as attributes and translate child events up into commands.
  Under this layout they belong to their context, not to the shell.
- **Templates & pages** — the shell's `index.html` and its router as
  it grows.

Everything renders in the light DOM: planks styles by tag-scoped
stylesheets injected into `document.head` (they would not pierce a
shadow root), and the design system follows the same convention.

## Run

```sh
<%= pm %> run dev        # Vite dev server for application/web-app
```

## Test

```sh
<%= pm %> test           # domain + fake contract tests in Node; design-system
                   # component tests under happy-dom
<%= pm %> run typecheck  # per-package tsc — enforces the lib walls
<%= pm %> run lint       # the walls resolution cannot hold
```

The emitted `.dependency-cruiser.cjs` carries an
`enhancedResolveOptions` block, and it is load-bearing rather than
tuning: with default options dependency-cruiser resolves every
`@<%= npmScope %>/*` import to a bare specifier, records no edge for
it, and reports zero violations over a tree that is in violation.

## Build

```sh
<%= pm %> run build      # application/web-app/dist — static files, ready to serve
```
